const CARDS_OPHTHO_RETINAL = [
{ q: "List the <b>10 layers of the retina</b> from outermost (closest to choroid) to innermost.", a: "<b>1.</b> Retinal pigment epithelium (RPE)<br><b>2.</b> Photoreceptor layer (rods & cones)<br><b>3.</b> External limiting membrane (ELM)<br><b>4.</b> Outer nuclear layer (ONL)<br><b>5.</b> Outer plexiform layer (OPL)<br><b>6.</b> Inner nuclear layer (INL)<br><b>7.</b> Inner plexiform layer (IPL)<br><b>8.</b> Ganglion cell layer (GCL)<br><b>9.</b> Nerve fiber layer (NFL)<br><b>10.</b> Internal limiting membrane (ILM)" },
{ q: "What is the <b>blood supply</b> to the outer retina vs the inner retina?", a: "<b>Outer retina</b> (RPE, photoreceptors, outer nuclear layer) → supplied by the <b>choroidal circulation</b> (short posterior ciliary arteries)<br><b>Inner retina</b> (ganglion cell layer, NFL, inner nuclear layer) → supplied by the <b>central retinal artery</b> (branch of ophthalmic artery from ICA)" },
{ q: "Why does the <b>fovea</b> appear as a <b>cherry-red spot</b> in CRAO?", a: "The fovea has <b>no inner retinal layers</b> — only photoreceptors — so it is supplied exclusively by the <b>choroidal circulation</b>, which remains perfused.<br>The surrounding pale retina reflects <b>ischemic edema of the inner retina</b> (supplied by the occluded central retinal artery), making the fovea appear red by contrast." },
{ q: "Define <b>macula</b>, <b>fovea</b>, <b>foveola</b>, and <b>umbo</b>.", a: "<b>Macula</b>: Central 5.5 mm area with xanthophyll pigment, responsible for central vision<br><b>Fovea</b>: Central depression within macula (~1.5 mm), highest cone density<br><b>Foveola</b>: Center of fovea (~0.35 mm), contains only cones, avascular<br><b>Umbo</b>: Light reflex at the very center of the foveola" },
{ q: "What constitutes the <b>blood-retinal barrier</b>?", a: "<b>Outer blood-retinal barrier</b>: Tight junctions of the <b>RPE</b> (separates retina from choroidal circulation)<br><b>Inner blood-retinal barrier</b>: Tight junctions of <b>retinal capillary endothelial cells</b> (non-fenestrated, similar to blood-brain barrier)" },
{ q: "Which retinal layers are contained in the <b>neurosensory retina</b> that detaches in an RD?", a: "All 9 layers from the <b>photoreceptor layer through the ILM</b>.<br>In a retinal detachment, the neurosensory retina separates from the underlying <b>RPE</b> at the level of the <b>subretinal space</b> (potential space between photoreceptors and RPE)." },
{ q: "Name the <b>three types</b> of retinal detachment.", a: "<b>1. Rhegmatogenous</b> (most common) — retinal break/tear allows liquefied vitreous into subretinal space<br><b>2. Tractional</b> — fibrovascular membranes pull retina away from RPE<br><b>3. Exudative (serous)</b> — fluid accumulates under retina without a break" },
{ q: "What is the <b>pathophysiology</b> of a <b>rhegmatogenous retinal detachment</b> (RRD)?", a: "A <b>full-thickness retinal break</b> (tear or hole) allows <b>liquefied vitreous humor</b> to pass through the break and accumulate in the <b>subretinal space</b>, separating the neurosensory retina from the RPE.<br>This is the <b>most common</b> type of retinal detachment." },
{ q: "List <b>risk factors</b> for rhegmatogenous retinal detachment.", a: "<b>• Myopia</b> (axial elongation thins retina)<br><b>• Prior cataract surgery / aphakia / pseudophakia</b><br><b>• Trauma</b><br><b>• Lattice degeneration</b><br><b>• Family history of RD</b><br><b>• Fellow eye RD</b> (risk ~10%)<br><b>• Age</b> (PVD more common with age)<br>• Marfan syndrome, Stickler syndrome" },
{ q: "What are the <b>classic symptoms</b> of rhegmatogenous retinal detachment?", a: "<b>1. Flashes</b> (photopsia) — vitreoretinal traction stimulating photoreceptors<br><b>2. Floaters</b> — pigment cells or blood in vitreous<br><b>3. Curtain / shadow / veil</b> spreading across visual field — progressing detachment<br><b>4. Painless</b> progressive visual field loss → central vision loss if macula detaches" },
{ q: "What is <b>Shafer sign</b> (tobacco dust)?", a: "<b>Pigmented cells</b> (from the RPE) seen floating in the <b>anterior vitreous</b> on slit lamp exam.<br>Highly suggestive of a <b>retinal tear or detachment</b>.<br>Also called <b>tobacco dust</b> due to the brown, granular appearance." },
{ q: "Differentiate <b>macula-on</b> vs <b>macula-off</b> retinal detachment in terms of urgency.", a: "<b>Macula-on RD</b>: Macula still attached → <b>surgical emergency within 24 hours</b> to preserve central vision<br><b>Macula-off RD</b>: Macula already detached → <b>urgent but not emergent</b>; surgery within days (visual prognosis already compromised)<br>Key: Duration of macular detachment correlates with final visual outcome." },
{ q: "Describe the <b>three surgical options</b> for rhegmatogenous retinal detachment.", a: "<b>1. Pneumatic retinopexy</b>: Intravitreal gas bubble + cryopexy/laser → for superior, single-break RD<br><b>2. Scleral buckle</b>: Silicone band sutured to sclera indents wall inward → closes break (good for inferior RD, young patients)<br><b>3. Pars plana vitrectomy (PPV)</b>: Remove vitreous, drain subretinal fluid, laser/cryo, gas/silicone oil tamponade → complex or posterior breaks" },
{ q: "In <b>pneumatic retinopexy</b>, what must the patient do postoperatively and why?", a: "The patient must maintain <b>specific head positioning</b> so the <b>gas bubble</b> (which rises) tamponades the retinal break.<br>Works best for <b>superior retinal breaks</b>.<br>Patient must <b>avoid air travel</b> until gas resorbs (gas expands at altitude → dangerous IOP rise)." },
{ q: "What is a <b>tractional retinal detachment</b>? Name causes.", a: "<b>Fibrovascular proliferation</b> creates membranes on the retinal surface that <b>contract and pull</b> the retina away from the RPE <b>without a retinal break</b>.<br><b>Causes:</b><br>• <b>Proliferative diabetic retinopathy</b> (most common)<br>• Sickle cell retinopathy<br>• Retinopathy of prematurity (ROP)<br>• Proliferative vitreoretinopathy (PVR)<br>• Penetrating trauma" },
{ q: "What is an <b>exudative (serous) retinal detachment</b>? List causes.", a: "Fluid accumulates in the subretinal space <b>without a retinal break or traction</b>.<br><b>Causes:</b><br>• <b>Vogt-Koyanagi-Harada (VKH)</b> syndrome<br>• <b>Preeclampsia / eclampsia</b><br>• Choroidal tumors (melanoma, metastases)<br>• Posterior scleritis<br>• Severe hypertension<br>• Central serous chorioretinopathy<br>• Coat disease<br>• Inflammatory conditions (sarcoid, sympathetic ophthalmia)" },
{ q: "How does a <b>tractional RD</b> appear differently from a rhegmatogenous RD on exam?", a: "<b>Tractional RD</b>: Retina has a <b>concave, taut, tent-like</b> configuration; does not extend to ora serrata; <b>no retinal break</b> visible<br><b>Rhegmatogenous RD</b>: Retina is <b>convex, corrugated, mobile</b>; <b>break/tear visible</b>; extends to ora serrata; subretinal fluid shifts with eye movement" },
{ q: "What is <b>posterior vitreous detachment</b> (PVD)?", a: "Age-related <b>liquefaction (syneresis)</b> of the vitreous gel causes it to <b>collapse and separate</b> from the retina (specifically from the ILM).<br>Usually begins posteriorly around the <b>optic disc and macula</b>.<br>Common after age <b>50–70</b>, earlier in myopes." },
{ q: "What is a <b>Weiss ring</b>?", a: "A <b>circular or oval ring of glial tissue</b> seen floating in the vitreous, representing the detached <b>peripapillary vitreous</b> (vitreous that was adherent to the optic disc margin).<br>It is <b>pathognomonic of a complete PVD</b>." },
{ q: "A patient presents with sudden <b>floaters and flashes</b>. What is the most important diagnosis to rule out?", a: "<b>Retinal tear</b> — present in ~<b>15%</b> of acute symptomatic PVDs.<br>All patients with new-onset flashes and floaters need a <b>dilated fundus exam</b> to rule out retinal tear/detachment.<br>If retinal tear found → prophylactic <b>laser photocoagulation</b> or <b>cryopexy</b> to prevent progression to RD." },
{ q: "How do <b>photopsias</b> (flashes) differ between PVD and migraine?", a: "<b>PVD flashes</b>: Brief, arc-like, <b>unilateral</b>, in temporal visual field, provoked by eye movement, due to vitreoretinal traction on photoreceptors<br><b>Migraine flashes</b>: Scintillating scotoma, <b>bilateral</b>, expanding zigzag lines (fortification spectra), last 15–30 min, may be followed by headache" },
{ q: "What is a <b>horseshoe (flap) tear</b> and why is it dangerous?", a: "A <b>U-shaped retinal tear</b> with a flap of retina pulled anteriorly by persistent vitreous traction.<br><b>Highest risk for progression to RRD</b> because:<br>• Ongoing vitreous traction keeps the flap open<br>• Liquefied vitreous easily enters the subretinal space<br><b>Treatment</b>: Prophylactic <b>laser photocoagulation</b> or <b>cryopexy</b> around the tear." },
{ q: "What is <b>lattice degeneration</b>?", a: "<b>Peripheral retinal thinning</b> with <b>crosshatched (lattice-like) white lines</b> representing sclerosed retinal vessels.<br>Found in <b>~8%</b> of the general population, <b>more common in myopes</b>.<br>Associated with atrophic holes and retinal tears → <b>risk factor for RRD</b>.<br>Usually no treatment unless symptomatic tears develop." },
{ q: "Differentiate <b>atrophic holes</b> from <b>horseshoe tears</b> in risk of retinal detachment.", a: "<b>Atrophic holes</b>: Round, no vitreous traction, <b>lower risk</b> for RD → usually <b>observed</b><br><b>Horseshoe tears</b>: Caused by vitreous traction, <b>higher risk</b> for RD → <b>require treatment</b> (laser or cryo)<br>Key: Traction = higher risk" },
{ q: "A patient presents with <b>sudden painless monocular vision loss</b>, counting-fingers vision, <b>APD</b>, and a <b>pale retina with a cherry-red spot</b>. Diagnosis?", a: "<b>Central retinal artery occlusion (CRAO)</b><br>This is an <b>ophthalmic emergency</b> — retinal ischemia causes irreversible damage within <b>~90 minutes</b>." },
{ q: "What is the <b>pathophysiology</b> of CRAO?", a: "<b>Occlusion of the central retinal artery</b> (branch of ophthalmic artery from ICA) → ischemia of the <b>inner retina</b>.<br><b>Causes:</b><br>• <b>Thromboembolism</b> from carotid atherosclerosis (most common)<br>• Cardiac emboli (valvular disease, AF)<br>• Giant cell arteritis (GCA)<br>• Hypercoagulable states<br>• Vasculitis, dissection" },
{ q: "Describe the <b>fundoscopic findings</b> in CRAO.", a: "<b>1. Pale/whitened retina</b> — ischemic edema of inner retinal layers<br><b>2. Cherry-red spot</b> at the macula (thin fovea lets choroidal red show through)<br><b>3. Box-car segmentation</b> — segmented blood column in retinal arterioles<br><b>4. Attenuated (narrowed) arterioles</b><br><b>5. Emboli may be visible</b> (Hollenhorst plaques)" },
{ q: "What are <b>Hollenhorst plaques</b>?", a: "<b>Cholesterol emboli</b> seen as <b>bright, refractile, yellow-orange</b> plaques at retinal arteriolar bifurcations.<br>Origin: <b>Carotid artery atherosclerosis</b> (usually ipsilateral ICA).<br>May be asymptomatic or cause transient/permanent visual loss." },
{ q: "Name the <b>types of retinal emboli</b> and their sources.", a: "<b>1. Cholesterol (Hollenhorst plaques)</b>: Bright yellow, refractile → <b>carotid atherosclerosis</b><br><b>2. Fibrin-platelet</b>: Dull white, elongated → <b>carotid or cardiac thrombus</b><br><b>3. Calcific</b>: White, non-refractile, often at disc → <b>cardiac valves</b> (aortic valve calcification)<br><b>4. Septic</b>: White, associated with Roth spots → <b>endocarditis</b>" },
{ q: "What is the <b>time window</b> for irreversible retinal damage in CRAO?", a: "<b>~90 minutes</b> of complete ischemia → irreversible retinal damage.<br>However, most patients present later.<br>Interventions may be attempted within <b>4.5–6 hours</b> of symptom onset (similar to stroke window)." },
{ q: "List the <b>acute treatments</b> for CRAO.", a: "<b>1. Ocular massage</b> — intermittent pressure to dislodge embolus<br><b>2. Anterior chamber paracentesis</b> — acute IOP reduction<br><b>3. IV acetazolamide</b> — reduce IOP<br><b>4. Carbogen inhalation</b> (95% O₂ + 5% CO₂) — vasodilate retinal vessels<br><b>5. Intra-arterial tPA</b> (within 4.5–6 h) — thrombolysis<br><b>6. Topical timolol</b> — reduce IOP<br>Note: Evidence for most treatments is limited." },
{ q: "Why must you <b>rule out GCA</b> in a patient >50 with CRAO?", a: "<b>GCA causes ~5% of CRAO</b> and can cause <b>bilateral blindness</b> if untreated.<br>Workup in all patients >50 with CRAO:<br>• <b>ESR</b> (often >50, sometimes >100)<br>• <b>CRP</b><br>• <b>CBC</b> (thrombocytosis, anemia)<br>If GCA suspected → <b>immediate high-dose IV methylprednisolone</b> → temporal artery biopsy (do NOT delay steroids for biopsy)." },
{ q: "What systemic <b>workup</b> is needed after CRAO?", a: "<b>1. Carotid Doppler ultrasound</b> — ipsilateral carotid stenosis<br><b>2. Echocardiogram</b> (TTE ± TEE) — cardiac emboli source<br><b>3. ECG / telemetry</b> — atrial fibrillation<br><b>4. ESR + CRP</b> — rule out GCA (age >50)<br><b>5. CBC, lipid panel, glucose, HbA1c</b> — vascular risk factors<br><b>6. Hypercoagulability workup</b> (young patients)" },
{ q: "What is a <b>branch retinal artery occlusion</b> (BRAO)?", a: "Occlusion of a <b>branch of the central retinal artery</b> → ischemia affecting only a <b>sector of the retina</b>.<br><b>Findings:</b> Sectoral pale retinal whitening corresponding to the affected branch; embolus often visible at bifurcation.<br><b>Visual field</b>: Corresponding altitudinal or sectoral defect.<br>Prognosis better than CRAO; same embolic workup required." },
{ q: "What is <b>cilioretinal artery sparing</b> in CRAO?", a: "~15–30% of people have a <b>cilioretinal artery</b> (from the choroidal/ciliary circulation, NOT the CRA).<br>In CRAO, this artery <b>remains patent</b>, preserving a small area of <b>papillomacular bundle perfusion</b> → relatively preserved central vision.<br>Seen as a <b>tongue of normal-colored retina</b> along the papillomacular bundle amid the pale ischemic retina." },
{ q: "What is <b>giant cell arteritis</b> (GCA / temporal arteritis)?", a: "A <b>granulomatous vasculitis</b> of <b>medium and large arteries</b>, especially branches of the <b>external carotid artery</b> (superficial temporal, ophthalmic arteries).<br>Affects patients <b>>50 years old</b> (mean age ~70).<br><b>Most common systemic vasculitis</b> in adults in the Western world." },
{ q: "List the <b>classic symptoms</b> of GCA.", a: "<b>• New-onset headache</b> (temporal, severe)<br><b>• Jaw claudication</b> (most specific symptom — pain with chewing)<br><b>• Scalp tenderness</b> (pain when combing hair)<br><b>• Constitutional symptoms</b> — fever, weight loss, malaise, fatigue<br><b>• Visual symptoms</b> — transient visual loss (amaurosis fugax), diplopia → permanent loss<br><b>• Polymyalgia rheumatica</b> (PMR) — in ~50% (proximal muscle pain/stiffness)" },
{ q: "What lab findings support a diagnosis of <b>GCA</b>?", a: "<b>ESR markedly elevated</b> — typically >50 mm/hr, often >100<br><b>CRP elevated</b> — more sensitive than ESR<br><b>Thrombocytosis</b> (reactive)<br><b>Normocytic anemia</b><br><b>Elevated alkaline phosphatase</b><br>Note: A <b>normal ESR does not rule out GCA</b> — CRP adds sensitivity." },
{ q: "What is the <b>gold standard</b> for diagnosing GCA?", a: "<b>Temporal artery biopsy</b><br>Shows: <b>Granulomatous inflammation</b> of the vessel wall with giant cells, intimal thickening, and fragmentation of the internal elastic lamina.<br><b>Skip lesions</b> are common → biopsy must be <b>≥2 cm in length</b>; if negative, contralateral biopsy may be needed.<br><b>Critical: Do NOT delay steroid treatment</b> while awaiting biopsy — biopsy remains positive for <b>up to 2 weeks</b> after starting steroids." },
{ q: "What is the <b>treatment</b> for GCA with visual symptoms?", a: "<b>Immediate high-dose IV methylprednisolone</b> (1 g/day × 3 days) → transition to <b>oral prednisone</b> (1 mg/kg/day) with slow taper over months.<br><b>Do NOT wait for biopsy results</b> before starting steroids.<br>Goal: Prevent <b>bilateral blindness</b> from anterior ischemic optic neuropathy (AION) or CRAO.<br>Tocilizumab (IL-6 inhibitor) as steroid-sparing agent." },
{ q: "What is the most feared <b>ophthalmic complication</b> of GCA?", a: "<b>Arteritic anterior ischemic optic neuropathy (A-AION)</b><br>Ischemia of the optic nerve head from occlusion of the <b>posterior ciliary arteries</b>.<br>Presents with <b>severe, sudden vision loss</b>, <b>disc edema</b> (pallid/chalky white), <b>APD</b>.<br>Without treatment, <b>fellow eye involvement occurs in ~50%</b> within days to weeks." },
{ q: "What is <b>central retinal vein occlusion</b> (CRVO)?", a: "Occlusion of the <b>central retinal vein</b> at or near the <b>lamina cribrosa</b> → impaired venous drainage → retinal congestion, hemorrhage, and edema.<br>Presents with <b>painless monocular vision loss</b> (less sudden than CRAO)." },
{ q: "Describe the <b>fundoscopic appearance</b> of CRVO.", a: "<b>'Blood and thunder' fundus:</b><br>• <b>Diffuse retinal hemorrhages</b> in all 4 quadrants (flame-shaped and dot-blot)<br>• <b>Cotton-wool spots</b> (nerve fiber layer infarcts)<br>• <b>Disc edema / hyperemia</b><br>• <b>Dilated, tortuous retinal veins</b><br>• <b>Macular edema</b> (most common cause of vision loss)" },
{ q: "List the <b>risk factors</b> for CRVO.", a: "<b>• Hypertension</b> (#1 risk factor)<br>• Diabetes mellitus<br>• Open-angle glaucoma / elevated IOP<br>• Hyperviscosity syndromes (polycythemia vera, Waldenström)<br>• Hypercoagulable states (Factor V Leiden, antiphospholipid syndrome)<br>• Oral contraceptives<br>• Vasculitis / inflammatory conditions<br>• Age >50" },
{ q: "Differentiate <b>non-ischemic (perfused)</b> vs <b>ischemic (non-perfused)</b> CRVO.", a: "<b>Non-ischemic (75%):</b><br>• Milder hemorrhages, better VA (often 20/200 or better)<br>• No APD, less cotton-wool spots<br>• <10 disc areas of capillary non-perfusion on FA<br>• Better prognosis; ~⅓ resolve spontaneously<br><br><b>Ischemic (25%):</b><br>• Severe hemorrhages, VA ≤20/200<br>• <b>APD present</b><br>• >10 disc areas of capillary non-perfusion on FA<br>• High risk of neovascularization → <b>neovascular glaucoma</b>" },
{ q: "What is <b>'100-day glaucoma'</b>?", a: "<b>Neovascular glaucoma (NVG)</b> developing ~<b>3 months (100 days)</b> after <b>ischemic CRVO</b>.<br>Mechanism: Retinal ischemia → VEGF release → <b>neovascularization of the iris (rubeosis iridis)</b> and angle → fibrovascular membrane blocks trabecular meshwork → secondary angle-closure glaucoma.<br>Occurs in ~<b>60%</b> of ischemic CRVO." },
{ q: "What is <b>rubeosis iridis</b>?", a: "<b>Neovascularization of the iris</b> — abnormal new blood vessels growing on the iris surface and into the anterior chamber angle.<br>Caused by <b>ischemia-driven VEGF</b> release (ischemic CRVO, proliferative DR, OIS, CRAO).<br>Leads to <b>neovascular glaucoma</b> (NVG) as fibrovascular tissue obstructs the trabecular meshwork." },
{ q: "What is the <b>treatment</b> for CRVO?", a: "<b>For macular edema:</b><br>• <b>Anti-VEGF injections</b> (ranibizumab, aflibercept, bevacizumab) — first-line<br>• Intravitreal dexamethasone implant (Ozurdex)<br><br><b>For neovascularization:</b><br>• <b>Panretinal photocoagulation (PRP)</b><br>• Anti-VEGF injections<br><br><b>Systemic:</b><br>• Treat underlying risk factors (HTN, DM, glaucoma)<br>• Hypercoagulability workup in young patients" },
{ q: "What is a <b>branch retinal vein occlusion</b> (BRVO)?", a: "Occlusion of a <b>branch of the retinal vein</b>, usually at an <b>arteriovenous (AV) crossing</b> where the artery compresses the vein.<br><b>Findings:</b> <b>Sectoral</b> (wedge-shaped) retinal hemorrhages, cotton-wool spots, and edema in the distribution of the affected branch.<br><b>Superotemporal branch</b> is most commonly affected." },
{ q: "Why does BRVO occur specifically at <b>AV crossings</b>?", a: "At AV crossings, the artery and vein share a common <b>adventitial sheath</b>.<br>Arteriosclerotic thickening of the artery <b>compresses the underlying vein</b> → turbulent flow → thrombus formation → venous occlusion.<br>This explains the strong association with <b>HTN</b> and <b>atherosclerosis</b>." },
{ q: "What key study demonstrated benefit of <b>anti-VEGF</b> for CRVO macular edema?", a: "The <b>CRUISE trial</b> (ranibizumab) and <b>COPERNICUS/GALILEO trials</b> (aflibercept) showed significant visual improvement with monthly anti-VEGF injections for macular edema secondary to CRVO.<br>Anti-VEGF is now <b>first-line therapy</b> for CRVO-associated macular edema." },
{ q: "What is a <b>vitreous hemorrhage</b>? How does it present?", a: "<b>Blood in the vitreous cavity</b>.<br><b>Symptoms:</b> Sudden onset of <b>floaters</b> (mild) or <b>profound vision loss</b> (dense), painless<br><b>Signs:</b><br>• <b>Loss of red reflex</b><br>• <b>Cannot visualize the fundus</b> (if dense)<br>• Blood may layer inferiorly" },
{ q: "List the <b>causes</b> of vitreous hemorrhage.", a: "<b>1. Proliferative diabetic retinopathy</b> (#1 cause overall)<br><b>2. PVD with retinal tear</b> (#1 cause in non-diabetics)<br><b>3. Retinal vein occlusion</b><br><b>4. Trauma</b><br><b>5. Terson syndrome</b> (vitreous hemorrhage from subarachnoid hemorrhage)<br><b>6. Sickle cell retinopathy</b><br><b>7. Retinal macroaneurysm</b><br><b>8. Age-related macular degeneration</b> (wet)<br><b>9. Valsalva retinopathy</b>" },
{ q: "What imaging is essential when the <b>fundus cannot be visualized</b> due to vitreous hemorrhage?", a: "<b>B-scan ultrasonography</b><br>Used to evaluate the posterior segment for:<br>• <b>Retinal detachment</b><br>• Retinal tear<br>• Posterior vitreous detachment<br>• Intraocular mass / tumor<br>Must rule out <b>RD</b>, which would require urgent surgical intervention." },
{ q: "What is the <b>treatment</b> for vitreous hemorrhage?", a: "<b>Mild/moderate (can see fundus):</b><br>• Observation — blood often clears spontaneously (weeks to months)<br>• Upright sleeping position (blood settles inferiorly)<br>• Treat underlying cause<br><br><b>Dense/non-clearing:</b><br>• <b>Pars plana vitrectomy (PPV)</b><br>• Also indicated if <b>associated retinal detachment</b><br>• Urgent PPV if RD suspected on B-scan" },
{ q: "What is <b>Terson syndrome</b>?", a: "<b>Vitreous hemorrhage</b> (or subhyaloid/preretinal hemorrhage) occurring in association with <b>subarachnoid hemorrhage (SAH)</b>.<br>Mechanism: Sudden rise in intracranial pressure → transmitted to optic nerve sheath → rupture of peripapillary retinal vessels.<br>Found in ~<b>10–20%</b> of SAH patients; associated with <b>worse neurological prognosis</b>." },
{ q: "What is <b>amaurosis fugax</b>?", a: "<b>Transient monocular vision loss</b> lasting <b>seconds to minutes</b>, described as a <b>curtain descending</b> over the eye, then lifting.<br>Caused by <b>transient retinal ischemia</b>, most commonly from <b>emboli</b> (carotid atherosclerosis).<br>Considered a <b>TIA equivalent</b> — high risk for future stroke." },
{ q: "What <b>workup</b> is indicated for amaurosis fugax?", a: "<b>1. Carotid Doppler ultrasound</b> — assess for ipsilateral carotid stenosis<br><b>2. Echocardiogram</b> (TTE ± TEE) — cardiac embolic source<br><b>3. ECG / Holter monitor</b> — atrial fibrillation<br><b>4. MRI brain / MRA</b> — stroke evaluation<br><b>5. CBC, lipid panel, glucose, ESR/CRP</b> (age >50 → rule out GCA)<br><b>6. Dilated fundoscopy</b> — Hollenhorst plaques, retinal findings" },
{ q: "How is <b>carotid stenosis</b> managed when found in amaurosis fugax?", a: "<b>Symptomatic carotid stenosis ≥70%:</b> <b>Carotid endarterectomy (CEA)</b> or carotid artery stenting<br><b>Symptomatic stenosis 50–69%:</b> CEA may benefit select patients<br><b><50%:</b> Medical management (antiplatelet, statin, risk factor control)<br>Also: <b>Aspirin or antiplatelet therapy</b> for all patients + statin + BP control" },
{ q: "Define <b>anterior ischemic optic neuropathy</b> (AION).", a: "Ischemia of the <b>optic nerve head</b> due to occlusion of the <b>short posterior ciliary arteries</b>.<br>Two types:<br><b>1. Arteritic (A-AION)</b> → caused by <b>GCA</b><br><b>2. Non-arteritic (NA-AION)</b> → caused by vasculopathic risk factors<br>Presents with <b>acute painless monocular vision loss</b> + <b>disc edema</b> + <b>altitudinal visual field defect</b>." },
{ q: "Compare <b>arteritic AION</b> (A-AION) vs <b>non-arteritic AION</b> (NA-AION).", a: "<b>A-AION (GCA):</b><br>• Age >70, severe vision loss (often CF or worse)<br>• Pallid/chalky disc edema<br>• ESR/CRP markedly elevated<br>• <b>Treat emergently with IV steroids</b><br>• High risk of fellow eye involvement<br><br><b>NA-AION:</b><br>• Age 40–60, moderate vision loss<br>• Hyperemic (not pallid) disc edema<br>• Vasculopathic risk factors (HTN, DM, hyperlipidemia)<br>• <b>'Disc at risk'</b> — small cup-to-disc ratio<br>• <b>No proven treatment</b>; manage risk factors" },
{ q: "What is the <b>'disc at risk'</b> in NA-AION?", a: "A <b>small, crowded optic disc</b> with a <b>small or absent physiologic cup</b> (cup-to-disc ratio <0.3).<br>The crowded optic nerve head predisposes to <b>compartment syndrome</b> — when axonal edema occurs, the tight disc cannot accommodate swelling → compression of capillaries → ischemia → NA-AION." },
{ q: "What visual field defect is characteristic of <b>AION</b>?", a: "<b>Altitudinal visual field defect</b> — typically <b>inferior altitudinal</b> (loss of the lower visual field), corresponding to ischemia of the <b>superior portion of the optic nerve head</b>.<br>May also be arcuate, central, or generalized." },
{ q: "What happens to the optic disc over time after AION?", a: "<b>Acutely:</b> <b>Disc edema</b> (pallid in A-AION, hyperemic in NA-AION)<br><b>4–6 weeks later:</b> Edema resolves → <b>optic disc pallor (atrophy)</b><br>The disc becomes pale and atrophic as damaged ganglion cell axons degenerate." },
{ q: "What is a <b>retinal artery macroaneurysm</b>?", a: "A <b>fusiform or saccular dilation</b> of a retinal arteriole (usually within the first 3 orders of bifurcation).<br><b>Demographics:</b> <b>Elderly hypertensive women</b><br><b>Location:</b> Superotemporal artery most common<br><b>Complications:</b> Hemorrhage (subretinal, intraretinal, preretinal, vitreous) or exudation (lipid deposition, macular edema)" },
{ q: "How is <b>retinal artery macroaneurysm</b> managed?", a: "<b>Observation</b> — many thrombose and involute spontaneously<br><b>Laser photocoagulation</b> — if associated with:<br>• Chronic macular edema threatening vision<br>• Circinate (ring) exudates encroaching on the fovea<br><b>Anti-VEGF</b> — for associated macular edema<br><b>Treat underlying HTN</b>" },
{ q: "What is <b>ocular ischemic syndrome</b> (OIS)?", a: "Chronic ocular hypoperfusion due to severe <b>ipsilateral carotid artery stenosis (>90%)</b> or occlusion.<br>Results from decreased flow through the <b>ophthalmic artery</b> → ischemia of all ocular structures." },
{ q: "List the <b>clinical features</b> of ocular ischemic syndrome.", a: "<b>Symptoms:</b> <b>Dull, aching periorbital pain</b>, gradual vision loss<br><b>Anterior segment:</b> Anterior chamber flare, rubeosis iridis, asymmetric cataract, <b>low IOP</b> (decreased aqueous production from ciliary body ischemia)<br><b>Posterior segment:</b><br>• <b>Dilated but non-tortuous veins</b> (key difference from CRVO)<br>• <b>Dot-blot hemorrhages in mid-periphery</b><br>• Microaneurysms<br>• Cotton-wool spots<br>• Spontaneous arterial pulsations on disc" },
{ q: "How do you differentiate <b>OIS</b> from <b>CRVO</b> on fundoscopy?", a: "<b>OIS:</b><br>• Veins <b>dilated but NOT tortuous</b><br>• Hemorrhages predominantly <b>mid-peripheral, dot-blot</b><br>• <b>Low IOP</b><br>• Spontaneous arterial pulsations<br><br><b>CRVO:</b><br>• Veins <b>dilated AND tortuous</b><br>• Hemorrhages <b>diffuse, all 4 quadrants</b> (flame + dot-blot)<br>• <b>Normal or elevated IOP</b><br>• No spontaneous arterial pulsations" },
{ q: "What finding helps distinguish OIS from diabetic retinopathy?", a: "<b>Asymmetric diabetic retinopathy</b> — more severe retinopathy on the side <b>contralateral</b> to the carotid stenosis (the ischemic eye has <b>less</b> perfusion pressure → fewer hemorrhages).<br>Also: <b>Low IOP</b> on the affected side and <b>neovascularization of the disc (NVD)</b> more common than NVE in OIS." },
{ q: "What is the <b>management</b> of ocular ischemic syndrome?", a: "<b>1. Carotid endarterectomy or stenting</b> — restore blood flow (if >70% stenosis)<br><b>2. PRP</b> — for neovascularization<br><b>3. Anti-VEGF</b> — for neovascularization/macular edema<br><b>4. Topical steroids + cycloplegics</b> — for anterior segment inflammation<br><b>5. IOP management</b> — if neovascular glaucoma develops<br><b>6. Systemic vascular risk factor control</b>" },
{ q: "What is a <b>Marcus Gunn pupil</b> (relative APD)?", a: "A <b>relative afferent pupillary defect</b> — the affected eye has <b>reduced direct pupillary response</b> compared to the consensual response.<br>Detected by the <b>swinging flashlight test</b>: When light swings to the affected eye, <b>both pupils dilate</b> (paradoxical dilation).<br>Indicates unilateral or asymmetric <b>optic nerve or extensive retinal disease</b>." },
{ q: "In which retinal conditions do you expect an <b>APD</b>?", a: "<b>CRAO</b> — present (extensive retinal ischemia)<br><b>Ischemic CRVO</b> — present (extensive retinal ischemia)<br><b>Non-ischemic CRVO</b> — usually absent<br><b>AION</b> — present (optic nerve ischemia)<br><b>Retinal detachment</b> — may be present (if extensive)<br><b>Optic neuritis</b> — present<br>Key: APD in CRVO suggests <b>ischemic type</b> → higher risk for NVG." },
{ q: "An elderly patient with CRVO develops <b>eye pain, red eye, and high IOP</b> 3 months later. What happened?", a: "<b>Neovascular glaucoma (NVG)</b> — '100-day glaucoma'<br>Retinal ischemia → VEGF → rubeosis iridis → fibrovascular membrane covers angle → <b>secondary angle-closure glaucoma</b><br>Treatment: Anti-VEGF + PRP + IOP-lowering (medical, then surgical if needed)" },
{ q: "What is <b>central serous chorioretinopathy</b> (CSCR)?", a: "Idiopathic serous detachment of the neurosensory retina at the <b>macula</b> due to leakage through the <b>RPE</b>.<br><b>Demographics:</b> Young men (30–50), type A personality, stress<br><b>Association:</b> Corticosteroid use, Cushing syndrome, pregnancy<br><b>Symptoms:</b> Central scotoma, metamorphopsia, micropsia, hyperopia shift<br><b>FA:</b> Classic <b>'smokestack'</b> or <b>'inkblot'</b> leakage pattern" },
{ q: "How does <b>CSCR</b> differ from exudative retinal detachment?", a: "<b>CSCR:</b> Small, dome-shaped, localized serous detachment at macula; no retinal break; young male; self-limited in most cases; associated with steroids<br><b>Exudative RD:</b> Larger, shifting fluid, associated with systemic disease (VKH, tumors, HTN, preeclampsia); no break; requires treatment of underlying cause" },
{ q: "What is <b>Vogt-Koyanagi-Harada</b> (VKH) disease?", a: "A <b>bilateral granulomatous panuveitis</b> with <b>exudative retinal detachments</b>, targeting <b>melanocyte-containing tissues</b>.<br><b>Features:</b><br>• <b>Bilateral exudative RD</b><br>• <b>Poliosis</b> (whitening of eyelashes/hair)<br>• <b>Vitiligo</b><br>• <b>Alopecia</b><br>• <b>Tinnitus / hearing loss</b><br>• <b>Meningismus</b> (CSF pleocytosis)<br>More common in darkly pigmented individuals (Asian, Hispanic, Native American)." },
{ q: "What retinal finding is associated with <b>preeclampsia/eclampsia</b>?", a: "<b>Bilateral exudative (serous) retinal detachments</b> — due to choroidal ischemia from arteriolar vasospasm.<br>Also: Arteriolar narrowing, retinal edema, cotton-wool spots, serous macular detachment.<br>Usually <b>resolves after delivery</b>.<br>Retinal detachment in pregnancy + HTN → think preeclampsia." },
{ q: "What is <b>proliferative vitreoretinopathy</b> (PVR)?", a: "The <b>most common cause of failure</b> of retinal detachment surgery.<br>RPE cells and glial cells proliferate and form <b>contractile membranes</b> on the retinal surface and in the vitreous → <b>tractional re-detachment</b> of the retina.<br>Risk factors: Multiple surgeries, large/chronic RD, vitreous hemorrhage, inflammation." },
{ q: "What are <b>cotton-wool spots</b>?", a: "<b>Nerve fiber layer infarcts</b> — ischemia causes <b>axoplasmic flow stasis</b> in retinal ganglion cell axons, forming fluffy white superficial lesions.<br><b>Seen in:</b><br>• CRVO, BRVO<br>• Hypertensive retinopathy<br>• Diabetic retinopathy<br>• HIV retinopathy<br>• Systemic lupus erythematosus<br>• OIS, CRAO (acutely — before diffuse whitening)" },
{ q: "What are <b>Roth spots</b>?", a: "<b>Retinal hemorrhages with white/pale centers</b>.<br>The white center represents <b>fibrin-platelet aggregates</b>.<br><b>Classically associated with:</b><br>• <b>Bacterial endocarditis</b> (most classic association)<br>• Leukemia<br>• Severe anemia<br>• Septic emboli<br>• Collagen vascular diseases" },
{ q: "What is <b>fluorescein angiography (FA)</b> and its role in retinal vascular disease?", a: "<b>IV fluorescein dye</b> → serial fundus photographs to evaluate retinal vasculature.<br><b>Uses:</b><br>• CRVO/BRVO: Quantify capillary non-perfusion areas (ischemic vs non-ischemic)<br>• Identify macular edema (leakage)<br>• Detect neovascularization (leakage from new vessels)<br>• CSCR: Smokestack/inkblot leakage pattern<br>• Diabetic retinopathy: Map ischemia and guide PRP" },
{ q: "What is <b>OCT</b> and how is it used in retinal emergencies?", a: "<b>Optical coherence tomography</b> — non-invasive cross-sectional imaging of the retina.<br><b>Uses:</b><br>• Detect and quantify <b>macular edema</b> (CRVO, BRVO, diabetic)<br>• Confirm <b>subretinal fluid</b> in RD, CSCR<br>• Monitor treatment response (anti-VEGF)<br>• Identify <b>epiretinal membranes</b>, <b>vitreomacular traction</b><br>• Evaluate <b>retinal layers</b> for ischemic damage" },
{ q: "What is the mechanism of <b>anti-VEGF therapy</b> in retinal vein occlusion?", a: "Retinal ischemia → release of <b>vascular endothelial growth factor (VEGF)</b> → <b>vascular permeability</b> (→ macular edema) + <b>neovascularization</b>.<br>Anti-VEGF agents (ranibizumab, aflibercept, bevacizumab) <b>bind and neutralize VEGF</b> → reduce macular edema and inhibit neovascularization.<br>Administered as <b>intravitreal injections</b>." },
{ q: "Name the commonly used <b>anti-VEGF agents</b>.", a: "<b>1. Ranibizumab (Lucentis)</b> — monoclonal antibody fragment against VEGF-A<br><b>2. Bevacizumab (Avastin)</b> — full monoclonal antibody (off-label, cost-effective)<br><b>3. Aflibercept (Eylea)</b> — VEGF-trap (binds VEGF-A, VEGF-B, PlGF)<br><b>4. Brolucizumab (Beovu)</b> — single-chain antibody fragment<br><b>5. Faricimab (Vabysmo)</b> — bispecific (anti-VEGF + anti-Ang-2)" },
{ q: "What is <b>panretinal photocoagulation (PRP)</b> and when is it used?", a: "<b>Laser burns applied to the peripheral retina</b> → destroys ischemic retina → <b>reduces VEGF production</b> → regresses/prevents neovascularization.<br><b>Indications:</b><br>• <b>Proliferative diabetic retinopathy</b><br>• <b>Ischemic CRVO</b> with neovascularization<br>• <b>Ocular ischemic syndrome</b> with NV<br>• <b>Neovascular glaucoma</b><br>Side effects: Peripheral visual field loss, decreased night vision" },
{ q: "A patient with diabetes has <b>sudden painless vision loss</b> and you cannot see the fundus. What is the most likely diagnosis and next step?", a: "<b>Most likely:</b> <b>Vitreous hemorrhage</b> from proliferative diabetic retinopathy<br><b>Next step:</b> <b>B-scan ultrasound</b> to:<br>• Rule out <b>retinal detachment</b> (tractional or combined tractional-rhegmatogenous)<br>• Assess extent of hemorrhage<br>If RD present → urgent PPV; if no RD → may observe, but most diabetic vitreous hemorrhages eventually need PPV." },
{ q: "What causes <b>flashes of light</b> (photopsia) in retinal disease?", a: "Flashes occur when the <b>retina is mechanically stimulated</b> — most commonly by <b>vitreoretinal traction</b>.<br><b>Causes:</b><br>• <b>PVD</b> (vitreous pulling on retina)<br>• <b>Retinal tear</b> (active traction)<br>• <b>Retinal detachment</b> (ongoing traction)<br>Photoreceptors cannot distinguish between light and mechanical stimulation → perceive traction as <b>flashes</b>." },
{ q: "What is the significance of <b>new-onset floaters</b> in an elderly patient?", a: "Most commonly due to <b>PVD</b> (benign), but must <b>rule out retinal tear</b> (~15% risk with acute PVD) and <b>vitreous hemorrhage</b>.<br><b>Floaters</b> in PVD are caused by vitreous opacities, condensation, or Weiss ring casting shadows on the retina.<br><b>Pigmented floaters (Shafer sign)</b> → suggests retinal tear/RD.<br><b>All patients need dilated fundoscopy within 24 hours.</b>" },
{ q: "What retinal conditions cause <b>painless</b> monocular vision loss?", a: "<b>1. CRAO</b> — sudden, severe<br><b>2. CRVO</b> — less sudden<br><b>3. Retinal detachment</b> — progressive curtain/shadow<br><b>4. Vitreous hemorrhage</b> — sudden floaters/haze<br><b>5. AION</b> — sudden, altitudinal field loss<br><b>6. Wet AMD</b> — subacute metamorphopsia<br><b>7. CSCR</b> — central scotoma, metamorphopsia<br><b>8. BRAO/BRVO</b> — sectoral field loss<br><b>Key:</b> Painful loss → think <b>acute angle-closure glaucoma, optic neuritis, endophthalmitis</b>" },
{ q: "How quickly must a <b>macula-on RRD</b> be repaired?", a: "<b>Within 24 hours</b> (ideally same day).<br>Once the macula detaches, central vision prognosis worsens significantly.<br>A <b>macula-off RD</b> is still urgent but can be repaired within <b>a few days</b> — the visual prognosis depends on duration of macular detachment." },
{ q: "What is the <b>prognosis for vision</b> after CRAO?", a: "<b>Poor</b> — most patients have final VA of <b>counting fingers or worse</b>.<br>Only ~<b>1–8%</b> achieve significant visual recovery.<br>Patients with a <b>cilioretinal artery</b> have better prognosis (central vision may be partially preserved).<br>Prognosis worse with: GCA-related CRAO, complete occlusion, delayed presentation." },
{ q: "What percentage of patients with PVD develop a <b>retinal tear</b>?", a: "Approximately <b>10–15%</b> of patients with acute symptomatic PVD develop a retinal tear.<br>Of those with retinal tears, ~<b>30–50%</b> will progress to retinal detachment if untreated.<br>This is why all patients with acute PVD symptoms need <b>dilated fundus examination</b>." },
{ q: "A patient presents with sudden vision loss. Fundus shows <b>pale retina, cherry-red spot, and box-car segmentation</b>. ESR is 95. Next step?", a: "<b>Immediately start high-dose IV methylprednisolone</b> (1 g/day) — this is <b>GCA-related CRAO</b> until proven otherwise.<br>Then: <b>Temporal artery biopsy</b> (but do NOT delay steroids).<br>Also: Urgent ophthalmology consultation for acute CRAO management.<br>Without treatment → risk of <b>fellow eye involvement</b> (CRAO or AION) within days." },
{ q: "In CRVO, what <b>fluorescein angiography finding</b> defines ischemic type?", a: "<b>>10 disc areas of capillary non-perfusion</b> on fluorescein angiography defines <b>ischemic CRVO</b>.<br>Other indicators of ischemic CRVO:<br>• VA ≤20/200<br>• APD present<br>• Dense hemorrhages<br>• Multiple cotton-wool spots<br>These patients need <b>close monitoring</b> for neovascularization." },
{ q: "What medications should be <b>avoided</b> in CSCR?", a: "<b>Corticosteroids</b> (all forms — systemic, topical, inhaled, intranasal, intra-articular)<br>Steroids <b>worsen</b> CSCR by increasing choroidal vascular permeability and RPE dysfunction.<br>Treatment: <b>Discontinue steroids</b> if possible → most cases resolve spontaneously in 1–3 months.<br>If persistent: <b>PDT (photodynamic therapy)</b> with verteporfin, or mineralocorticoid receptor antagonists (eplerenone/spironolactone)." },
{ q: "What retinal finding is <b>pathognomonic</b> for hypertensive emergency (malignant HTN)?", a: "<b>Bilateral disc edema (papilledema)</b> with:<br>• Flame-shaped hemorrhages<br>• Cotton-wool spots<br>• Hard exudates (macular star)<br>• Arteriolar narrowing / AV nicking<br>• Exudative retinal detachments (in severe cases)<br>This is <b>grade IV hypertensive retinopathy</b> (Keith-Wagener-Barker classification)." },
{ q: "What retinal finding occurs with <b>sickle cell retinopathy</b>?", a: "<b>Proliferative sickle retinopathy</b> → <b>sea fan neovascularization</b> (fan-shaped NV at junction of perfused and non-perfused peripheral retina).<br>Stages (Goldberg): I. Peripheral arteriolar occlusion → II. AV anastomoses → III. Sea fan NV → IV. Vitreous hemorrhage → V. Tractional RD.<br><b>SC disease and S-Thal</b> have worse retinopathy than SS (better perfusion → more ischemia-reperfusion injury)." },
{ q: "How does <b>retinopathy of prematurity (ROP)</b> cause retinal detachment?", a: "In ROP, <b>abnormal neovascularization</b> at the junction of vascularized and avascular peripheral retina → <b>fibrovascular proliferation</b> → contraction of fibrovascular tissue → <b>tractional retinal detachment</b>.<br><b>Risk factors:</b> Prematurity (<32 wk, <1500 g), supplemental oxygen<br><b>Screening:</b> All infants ≤30 wk GA or ≤1500 g birth weight<br><b>Treatment:</b> Laser to avascular retina, anti-VEGF, vitrectomy if RD" },
{ q: "What is the <b>Valsalva retinopathy</b>?", a: "Sudden <b>premacular (subhyaloid) hemorrhage</b> caused by a sharp increase in intravenous pressure during <b>Valsalva maneuver</b> (straining, coughing, vomiting, heavy lifting).<br>Blood trapped between retina and posterior hyaloid → <b>dome-shaped, boat-shaped</b> hemorrhage at the macula.<br><b>Treatment:</b> Usually self-limited; Nd:YAG laser membranotomy if threatening macula long-term." },
{ q: "What is an <b>Amsler grid</b> and its clinical significance?", a: "A grid of <b>evenly spaced horizontal and vertical lines</b> used to detect <b>central visual field abnormalities</b>.<br>Patient fixes on center dot and reports any:<br>• <b>Metamorphopsia</b> (wavy lines) → macular disease (AMD, ERM, CSCR)<br>• <b>Scotomas</b> (missing areas) → macular pathology<br>Used for <b>home monitoring</b> in patients with AMD (detect conversion to wet AMD)." },
{ q: "A patient with a history of <b>lattice degeneration</b> presents with a <b>horseshoe tear</b> found on exam but no RD. What is the treatment?", a: "<b>Prophylactic laser photocoagulation (barricade laser)</b> or <b>cryopexy</b> around the tear.<br>Creates a <b>chorioretinal adhesion</b> (scar) that walls off the tear and prevents subretinal fluid from entering.<br>Horseshoe tears have <b>high risk of progressing to RRD</b> due to ongoing vitreous traction.<br>Patient should return <b>immediately</b> if new symptoms (floaters, flashes, curtain) develop." },
{ q: "What is the role of <b>cryopexy</b> in retinal tear management?", a: "<b>Transcleral cryotherapy</b> applied externally to freeze and destroy tissue around the retinal break → creates a <b>chorioretinal adhesion</b> (scar) sealing the break.<br>Used as alternative to laser when:<br>• Media opacities prevent laser (e.g., vitreous hemorrhage, cataract)<br>• Break is very anterior (ora serrata)<br>• During scleral buckle surgery<br>Takes ~1–2 weeks for full adhesion to form." },
{ q: "What is <b>scleral buckle surgery</b> and how does it work?", a: "A <b>silicone band or sponge</b> is sutured to the <b>external sclera</b>, creating an inward <b>indentation (buckle)</b> that:<br>1. Brings the RPE closer to the detached retina<br>2. Relieves vitreoretinal traction<br>3. Supports the retinal break closure<br>Combined with cryopexy or laser to seal the break.<br><b>Best for:</b> Young patients, inferior RDs, single breaks, phakic patients." },
{ q: "What are the <b>complications</b> of scleral buckle surgery?", a: "<b>• Myopic shift</b> (increased axial length from indentation)<br><b>• Diplopia</b> (extraocular muscle dysfunction)<br><b>• Infection / extrusion</b> of buckle material<br><b>• Anterior segment ischemia</b><br><b>• Choroidal detachment</b><br><b>• Cystoid macular edema</b><br><b>• Proliferative vitreoretinopathy (PVR)</b>" },
{ q: "What is <b>pars plana vitrectomy</b> (PPV)?", a: "Surgical removal of the <b>vitreous gel</b> through small-gauge instruments inserted through the <b>pars plana</b> (3.5–4 mm posterior to the limbus).<br>Allows:<br>• Removal of vitreous traction<br>• Drainage of subretinal fluid<br>• Endolaser / cryopexy to seal breaks<br>• Gas or silicone oil tamponade<br><b>Indications:</b> Complex RD, GRT, vitreous hemorrhage, macular hole, ERM, tractional RD" },
{ q: "After PPV with <b>gas tamponade</b>, what must patients avoid?", a: "<b>1. Air travel</b> — gas expands at altitude → dangerous IOP elevation → can cause CRAO<br><b>2. Nitrous oxide anesthesia</b> — N₂O diffuses into gas bubble → rapid expansion → IOP spike<br><b>3. Must maintain specific head positioning</b> (face-down for macular holes)<br>Gas types:<br>• SF₆ (sulfur hexafluoride) — lasts ~2 weeks<br>• C₃F₈ (perfluoropropane) — lasts ~8 weeks" },
{ q: "What is <b>silicone oil tamponade</b> and when is it preferred over gas?", a: "<b>Silicone oil</b> injected into the vitreous cavity as a long-term tamponade agent.<br><b>Preferred over gas when:</b><br>• Patient requires <b>air travel</b><br>• <b>Inferior retinal detachments</b> (gas floats superiorly)<br>• Complex cases with <b>PVR</b><br>• Pediatric patients (unable to position)<br><b>Disadvantage:</b> Requires a <b>second surgery</b> for removal; can cause cataract, glaucoma, keratopathy." },
{ q: "What are the <b>indications for urgent/emergent dilated fundus exam</b>?", a: "<b>1. New-onset floaters and/or flashes</b> (rule out tear/RD)<br><b>2. Sudden painless vision loss</b> (CRAO, CRVO, VH, RD, AION)<br><b>3. Curtain/shadow over visual field</b> (RD)<br><b>4. Trauma with decreased vision</b><br><b>5. Shafer sign on slit lamp</b> (pigmented vitreous cells)<br><b>6. New APD</b><br>Time-critical: <b>Macula-on RD</b> (24h), <b>CRAO</b> (90 min)" },
{ q: "What <b>systemic conditions</b> can cause <b>exudative retinal detachment</b>?", a: "<b>1. Preeclampsia / eclampsia</b><br><b>2. Malignant hypertension</b><br><b>3. VKH disease</b><br><b>4. Posterior scleritis</b><br><b>5. Choroidal tumors</b> (melanoma, metastases, hemangioma)<br><b>6. Coats disease</b> (children)<br><b>7. Sympathetic ophthalmia</b><br><b>8. Leukemia</b><br><b>9. Organ transplant rejection</b> (choroidal)<br>Key: <b>No retinal break</b>, fluid shifts with gravity" },
{ q: "What is <b>sympathetic ophthalmia</b>?", a: "A <b>bilateral granulomatous panuveitis</b> that occurs after <b>penetrating trauma or surgery</b> to one eye.<br><b>Mechanism:</b> Autoimmune T-cell response against uveal melanocyte antigens exposed by the injury.<br><b>Exciting eye</b> = injured eye; <b>sympathizing eye</b> = uninjured fellow eye (develops uveitis).<br>Can cause <b>bilateral exudative RD</b>.<br><b>Treatment:</b> Systemic immunosuppression; consider enucleation of exciting eye <b>within 2 weeks</b> of injury." },
{ q: "What is the difference between <b>optic neuritis</b> and <b>AION</b>?", a: "<b>Optic neuritis:</b><br>• <b>Painful</b> vision loss (pain with eye movement)<br>• Young women (20–40)<br>• Associated with <b>MS</b><br>• Disc may be normal (retrobulbar) or edematous<br>• Good visual recovery<br><br><b>AION:</b><br>• <b>Painless</b> vision loss<br>• Older patients (>50)<br>• Altitudinal field defect<br>• Disc edema (pallid or hyperemic)<br>• Variable recovery (worse with arteritic)" },
{ q: "What is <b>papilledema</b> vs <b>papillitis</b>?", a: "<b>Papilledema:</b> Bilateral disc edema from <b>raised intracranial pressure</b>; usually <b>preserved VA</b> initially; enlarged blind spot<br><b>Papillitis:</b> Disc edema from <b>inflammation</b> (optic neuritis); usually <b>decreased VA</b> with APD; typically unilateral; painful eye movements<br>Both show disc edema but have different etiologies and presentations." },
{ q: "What is <b>Coat disease</b>?", a: "A <b>unilateral, non-hereditary</b> retinal vascular disorder of <b>young boys</b> (average age 5–10).<br>Features: <b>Telangiectatic retinal vessels</b> → lipid exudation → exudative retinal detachment.<br>Presents with <b>leukocoria</b> (white pupil) → must differentiate from <b>retinoblastoma</b>.<br>No systemic associations.<br>Treatment: Laser or cryotherapy to abnormal vessels." },
{ q: "List the <b>differential diagnosis of leukocoria</b> in a child.", a: "<b>1. Retinoblastoma</b> (#1 concern — rule out first)<br><b>2. Coats disease</b><br><b>3. Retinopathy of prematurity (ROP)</b><br><b>4. Persistent fetal vasculature (PFV)</b><br><b>5. Toxocara canis</b><br><b>6. Familial exudative vitreoretinopathy (FEVR)</b><br><b>7. Cataract (congenital)</b><br><b>8. Retinal detachment</b><br><b>9. Coloboma</b><br><b>10. Norrie disease</b>" },
{ q: "What is a <b>giant retinal tear</b> (GRT)?", a: "A retinal tear extending <b>≥90° (3 clock hours)</b> of the circumference.<br><b>Associations:</b> Marfan syndrome, Stickler syndrome, high myopia, trauma<br>The retinal flap can fold over on itself → difficult to repair.<br><b>Treatment:</b> PPV with perfluorocarbon liquid (to unfold retina) → endolaser → silicone oil or gas tamponade." },
{ q: "What visual field pattern is expected in a <b>superior RD</b>?", a: "<b>Inferior visual field defect</b> — the visual field loss is <b>opposite</b> to the location of the detachment (inverted image on retina).<br>A patient with superior RD reports a <b>curtain rising from below</b>.<br>Superior RDs are more <b>concerning</b> because they progress toward the macula faster (gravity assists subretinal fluid tracking downward along the retina)." },
{ q: "Why is a <b>superior retinal tear</b> more dangerous than an inferior one?", a: "Gravity causes <b>subretinal fluid to track downward</b> from a superior tear, rapidly progressing toward the <b>macula</b>.<br>An <b>inferior tear</b> has fluid that tends to stay inferior, and progression toward the macula is slower.<br>This is why <b>superior RDs threaten the macula faster</b> and may need more urgent repair." },
{ q: "What is <b>proliferative diabetic retinopathy</b> (PDR) and how does it relate to retinal emergencies?", a: "PDR features <b>neovascularization</b> (NVD — disc; NVE — elsewhere) from severe retinal ischemia/VEGF release.<br><b>Emergencies from PDR:</b><br>• <b>Vitreous hemorrhage</b> (from NV bleed)<br>• <b>Tractional retinal detachment</b> (fibrovascular contraction)<br>• <b>Combined tractional-rhegmatogenous RD</b><br>• <b>Neovascular glaucoma</b> (rubeosis iridis)<br><b>Treatment:</b> PRP, anti-VEGF, PPV" },
{ q: "A patient with known <b>ischemic CRVO</b> returns with a <b>red, painful eye and IOP of 55 mmHg</b>. What do you see on slit lamp?", a: "<b>Rubeosis iridis</b> — neovascularization of the iris → <b>neovascular glaucoma</b>.<br><b>Slit lamp findings:</b><br>• Fine, irregular blood vessels on the <b>iris surface and pupillary margin</b><br>• <b>Ectropion uveae</b> (pupillary margin everted by fibrovascular membrane)<br>• <b>Synechiae</b> — angle closure from fibrovascular membrane<br>• <b>Corneal edema</b> from elevated IOP<br>• Conjunctival injection, flare/cells" },
{ q: "What is the <b>Cherry Red Spot Myoclonus</b> syndrome?", a: "A cherry-red spot can also be seen in <b>lysosomal storage diseases</b> (not just CRAO):<br>• <b>Tay-Sachs disease</b> (GM2 gangliosidosis, type I) — most classic<br>• <b>Niemann-Pick disease</b> (type A)<br>• <b>Sandhoff disease</b><br>• <b>GM1 gangliosidosis</b><br>• <b>Sialidosis</b> (cherry-red spot myoclonus)<br>In these, <b>lipid accumulation in ganglion cells</b> turns the retina pale, with the foveola (no ganglion cells) appearing red." },
{ q: "How does <b>acute CRAO presentation</b> differ from <b>acute CRVO presentation</b>?", a: "<b>CRAO:</b><br>• <b>Sudden, severe</b> (seconds to minutes)<br>• VA usually CF or worse<br>• <b>Pale retina, cherry-red spot</b><br>• Attenuated arterioles, box-car segmentation<br>• APD present<br><br><b>CRVO:</b><br>• <b>Less sudden</b> (hours to days)<br>• VA variable (20/40 to CF)<br>• <b>Blood and thunder</b> — hemorrhages in all quadrants<br>• Dilated, tortuous veins, disc edema<br>• APD only in ischemic type" },
{ q: "What happens if a patient with a <b>gas bubble</b> in the eye receives <b>nitrous oxide</b> anesthesia?", a: "<b>Nitrous oxide (N₂O)</b> is <b>34× more soluble</b> than SF₆ in blood → rapidly <b>diffuses into the gas bubble</b> → <b>dramatic expansion</b> of the bubble → <b>acute IOP elevation</b> → can cause <b>CRAO and permanent vision loss</b>.<br><b>N₂O must be stopped ≥15 minutes before</b> and <b>avoided for 2 weeks (SF₆) to 8 weeks (C₃F₈)</b> after surgery.<br>Patients should carry a <b>medical alert</b> bracelet." },
{ q: "What is the <b>most common cause</b> of painless monocular vision loss in patients <b>over 50</b>?", a: "In terms of retinal emergencies:<br>• <b>Retinal vein occlusion</b> (CRVO/BRVO) — most common retinal vascular cause<br>• <b>Age-related macular degeneration</b> (wet) — most common overall cause of permanent vision loss in elderly<br>In terms of transient loss: <b>Amaurosis fugax</b> from carotid disease" },
{ q: "What is the <b>prognosis</b> of non-ischemic vs ischemic CRVO?", a: "<b>Non-ischemic CRVO:</b><br>• ~⅓ improve spontaneously<br>• ~⅓ remain stable<br>• ~⅓ convert to ischemic type<br>• Good visual outcome with anti-VEGF for macular edema<br><br><b>Ischemic CRVO:</b><br>• Poor visual prognosis<br>• ~60% develop NVG within 3 months<br>• Need close follow-up (monthly) for first 6 months<br>• May need PRP if NV develops" },
{ q: "What is the role of <b>intravitreal steroid implants</b> in CRVO?", a: "<b>Dexamethasone intravitreal implant (Ozurdex)</b>:<br>• Sustained-release biodegradable implant<br>• FDA-approved for macular edema from CRVO/BRVO<br>• Alternative to anti-VEGF, especially in <b>pseudophakic</b> patients<br>• Lasts ~3–6 months per injection<br><b>Side effects:</b> Cataract progression, IOP elevation<br>Generally <b>second-line</b> after anti-VEGF." },
{ q: "What is <b>Eales disease</b>?", a: "An <b>idiopathic perivasculitis</b> of the <b>peripheral retinal veins</b>, predominantly in <b>young adult males</b> (20–30) from the Indian subcontinent.<br><b>Stages:</b><br>1. Inflammatory (periphlebitis)<br>2. Ischemic (peripheral capillary non-perfusion)<br>3. Proliferative (neovascularization → vitreous hemorrhage)<br><b>Treatment:</b> Steroids (inflammation), laser PRP (ischemia/NV), PPV (vitreous hemorrhage)" },
{ q: "What is the <b>Irvine-Gass syndrome</b>?", a: "<b>Pseudophakic cystoid macular edema (CME)</b> — developing <b>4–12 weeks after cataract surgery</b>.<br>Most common cause of decreased vision after uncomplicated cataract surgery.<br><b>Mechanism:</b> Surgical inflammation → prostaglandin release → breakdown of blood-retinal barrier → macular edema.<br><b>Treatment:</b> Topical NSAIDs ± topical steroids; anti-VEGF or intravitreal steroids for refractory cases." },
{ q: "What is <b>macular hole</b> and how does it relate to PVD?", a: "<b>Full-thickness defect in the foveal neurosensory retina</b>.<br><b>Mechanism:</b> <b>Vitreomacular traction</b> (incomplete PVD with persistent foveal adhesion) → tangential traction → hole formation.<br><b>Symptoms:</b> Central scotoma, metamorphopsia, decreased VA<br><b>OCT:</b> Full-thickness foveal defect<br><b>Treatment:</b> PPV + ILM peeling + gas tamponade + face-down positioning" },
{ q: "What is <b>epiretinal membrane</b> (ERM / macular pucker)?", a: "A <b>thin fibrocellular membrane</b> on the surface of the <b>ILM</b> at the macula.<br><b>Cause:</b> Often idiopathic (after PVD), also from surgery, trauma, inflammation, RVO.<br><b>Symptoms:</b> Metamorphopsia (distortion), blurred central vision<br><b>OCT:</b> Hyperreflective line on retinal surface ± macular wrinkling/thickening<br><b>Treatment:</b> PPV + membrane peel (if visually significant)" },
{ q: "What <b>pupil findings</b> differentiate a <b>complete vs incomplete CRAO</b>?", a: "<b>Complete CRAO:</b><br>• <b>APD (Marcus Gunn pupil)</b> — fixed, dilated pupil with poor direct response<br>• Near-complete loss of retinal function<br><br><b>Incomplete / BRAO:</b><br>• APD may be <b>subtle or absent</b> (depends on extent)<br>• Some retinal function preserved<br><br>A <b>sluggish but reactive pupil</b> suggests <b>incomplete</b> occlusion or cilioretinal artery sparing." },
{ q: "What is <b>photodynamic therapy (PDT)</b> with verteporfin?", a: "<b>Verteporfin</b> (IV photosensitizing dye) accumulates in abnormal vasculature → activated by <b>non-thermal laser (689 nm)</b> → generates reactive oxygen species → selective <b>thrombosis of abnormal vessels</b>.<br><b>Uses:</b><br>• <b>Wet AMD</b> (subfoveal choroidal NV — largely replaced by anti-VEGF)<br>• <b>Chronic CSCR</b> (reduces choroidal hyperpermeability)<br>• Choroidal hemangioma<br>• Polypoidal choroidal vasculopathy" },
{ q: "What is <b>VEGF</b> and why is it central to retinal vascular disease?", a: "<b>Vascular endothelial growth factor</b> — a signal protein that promotes:<br>• <b>Angiogenesis</b> (new vessel formation)<br>• <b>Vascular permeability</b> (edema)<br>Released by <b>ischemic retinal cells</b> in response to hypoxia.<br><b>Drives:</b> Neovascularization (PDR, RVO, ROP), macular edema (DME, RVO), neovascular glaucoma.<br>Targeted by anti-VEGF therapy (ranibizumab, aflibercept, bevacizumab)." },
{ q: "How do you differentiate <b>retinal hemorrhages</b> by shape?", a: "<b>Flame-shaped (splinter):</b> In the <b>nerve fiber layer</b> (superficial) → spread along nerve fibers → seen in HTN, CRVO, papilledema<br><b>Dot-blot:</b> In the <b>inner nuclear / outer plexiform layer</b> (deep) → round → seen in DM, CRVO, OIS<br><b>Subhyaloid (preretinal):</b> Between retina and vitreous → boat/dome-shaped, gravity-dependent → PDR, Terson, Valsalva<br><b>Sub-RPE / subretinal:</b> Dark, well-demarcated → wet AMD, macroaneurysm" },
{ q: "What is <b>optic disc neovascularization (NVD)</b> vs <b>neovascularization elsewhere (NVE)</b>?", a: "<b>NVD:</b> New vessels at or within <b>1 disc diameter of the optic disc</b> → <b>higher risk</b> of vitreous hemorrhage; more aggressive treatment indicated<br><b>NVE:</b> New vessels <b>elsewhere on the retina</b> → lower risk but still requires treatment<br><b>High-risk characteristics (DRS):</b> NVD ≥¼ disc area, any NVD with VH, NVE ≥½ disc area with VH → <b>PRP indicated</b>" },
{ q: "What <b>medications</b> can cause retinal toxicity?", a: "<b>1. Hydroxychloroquine (Plaquenil)</b> → bull's eye maculopathy (RPE damage)<br><b>2. Tamoxifen</b> → crystalline maculopathy, macular edema<br><b>3. Thioridazine</b> → pigmentary retinopathy<br><b>4. Chloroquine</b> → bull's eye maculopathy<br><b>5. Ethambutol</b> → optic neuritis (not retinal per se)<br><b>6. Vigabatrin</b> → irreversible peripheral field constriction<br><b>7. Interferon</b> → cotton-wool spots, retinal hemorrhages<br><b>8. Topiramate</b> → angle-closure (not retinal)" },
{ q: "What is the <b>difference between endophthalmitis and retinal emergency</b> in terms of presentation?", a: "<b>Endophthalmitis:</b><br>• <b>Painful</b> vision loss<br>• Red eye, hypopyon, vitritis<br>• Usually post-surgical or post-traumatic<br>• Infection inside the eye<br><br><b>Retinal emergencies (CRAO, CRVO, RD):</b><br>• <b>Painless</b> vision loss (except OIS — dull ache)<br>• White/quiet eye externally<br>• Specific fundoscopic findings<br>Key: <b>Pain + vision loss + red eye = consider endophthalmitis</b>" },
{ q: "In the context of retinal detachment, what is a <b>demarcation line</b>?", a: "A <b>pigmented line</b> at the border of a <b>chronic, stable retinal detachment</b>, representing <b>RPE hyperplasia</b> at the junction of attached and detached retina.<br>Indicates the RD has been present for <b>weeks to months</b> and is <b>not actively progressing</b>.<br>May be observed if macula is not threatened, but most RDs still warrant repair." },
{ q: "What is a <b>dialysis</b> in the context of retinal detachment?", a: "A <b>retinal tear at the ora serrata</b> (junction of retina and pars plana), running <b>circumferentially</b>.<br><b>Causes:</b> Blunt <b>trauma</b> (especially inferotemporal), idiopathic (especially superonasal in young adults)<br><b>Significance:</b> Common cause of RRD in <b>young patients</b> after trauma.<br><b>Treatment:</b> Scleral buckle (preferred) or PPV." },
{ q: "What is the <b>ora serrata</b>?", a: "The <b>serrated anterior border</b> of the retina, where the sensory retina ends and transitions into the <b>pars plana</b> of the ciliary body.<br>Important landmark for:<br>• Retinal detachment (RD extends to ora serrata in RRD)<br>• Retinal dialysis (tear at ora serrata)<br>• Pars plana vitrectomy approach (instruments inserted posterior to ora serrata)" },
{ q: "What is <b>hemi-retinal vein occlusion</b> (HRVO)?", a: "Occlusion involving <b>either the superior or inferior trunk</b> of the central retinal vein (which bifurcates within the optic nerve).<br>Features are <b>intermediate</b> between CRVO and BRVO — hemorrhages involving <b>2 quadrants</b> (superior or inferior half).<br>Same risk factors as CRVO; managed similarly with anti-VEGF for macular edema and PRP if NV develops." },
{ q: "What <b>fundus finding</b> differentiates <b>papilledema</b> from <b>optic disc drusen</b>?", a: "<b>Papilledema:</b><br>• Blurred disc margins, hyperemic disc<br>• <b>Obscured peripapillary vessels</b><br>• Flame hemorrhages, cotton-wool spots<br>• <b>Absent spontaneous venous pulsations</b><br>• Enlarged blind spot on VF<br><br><b>Optic disc drusen:</b><br>• Lumpy, irregular disc elevation<br>• <b>No vessel obscuration</b><br>• <b>Autofluorescence</b> on FAF<br>• Normal spontaneous venous pulsations usually present<br>• B-scan: calcification" },
{ q: "How does <b>myopia</b> predispose to retinal detachment?", a: "<b>Axial elongation</b> in myopia causes:<br>1. <b>Retinal thinning</b> (stretched over larger globe)<br>2. <b>Lattice degeneration</b> more common<br>3. <b>Earlier PVD</b> (vitreous liquefaction)<br>4. <b>Weaker vitreoretinal adhesion</b> in some areas<br>5. <b>Peripheral retinal degenerations</b><br>Risk increases with degree of myopia: >6 diopters = <b>high risk</b>." },
{ q: "What is <b>Stickler syndrome</b> and its retinal significance?", a: "<b>Autosomal dominant connective tissue disorder</b> (type II collagen defect).<br><b>Features:</b> Midface hypoplasia, cleft palate, sensorineural hearing loss, joint hypermobility, high myopia.<br><b>Ocular:</b><br>• <b>Optically empty vitreous</b> (membranous or beaded vitreous)<br>• <b>High risk of retinal detachment</b> (giant retinal tears)<br>• <b>Early-onset lattice degeneration</b><br>• Cataracts (often early)<br>Prophylactic retinal laser may be considered." },
{ q: "A 25-year-old with <b>sickle cell disease</b> (HbSC) has <b>sudden floaters and decreased vision</b>. Most likely cause?", a: "<b>Vitreous hemorrhage</b> from <b>proliferative sickle cell retinopathy</b>.<br><b>HbSC disease</b> has <b>worse retinopathy</b> than HbSS because better-oxygenated blood allows more perfusion → more <b>ischemia-reperfusion injury</b> at the periphery → sea fan NV → hemorrhage.<br><b>Management:</b> B-scan US (rule out RD), observation if mild, PPV if dense/non-clearing or RD present. Avoid scleral buckle (risk of anterior segment ischemia in SCD)." },
{ q: "What is <b>Purtscher retinopathy</b>?", a: "A retinopathy characterized by <b>multiple cotton-wool spots and retinal hemorrhages</b> surrounding the disc, occurring after <b>severe trauma</b> (especially chest compression, long bone fractures, head injury).<br><b>Mechanism:</b> Complement-mediated arteriolar occlusion or fat embolism → retinal ischemia.<br><b>Purtscher-like retinopathy:</b> Similar findings in <b>acute pancreatitis, SLE, TTP, renal failure</b>.<br>Usually self-limited; no specific treatment." },
{ q: "What is an <b>operculated hole</b> in the retina?", a: "A <b>round retinal hole</b> with a free-floating <b>operculum</b> (plug of retinal tissue) in the vitreous above it.<br>The vitreous traction has <b>completely avulsed</b> the retinal fragment → <b>no residual traction</b> on the hole.<br>Therefore, <b>lower risk of RD</b> than horseshoe tears (which have ongoing traction).<br>Usually <b>observed</b>; treatment only if symptomatic or high-risk features." },
{ q: "What conditions are associated with <b>bilateral simultaneous CRAO</b>?", a: "<b>1. Giant cell arteritis (GCA)</b> — can affect bilateral posterior ciliary and retinal arteries<br><b>2. Cardiac emboli</b> (bilateral shower of emboli)<br><b>3. Hypercoagulable states</b> (antiphospholipid syndrome, DIC)<br><b>4. Vasculitis</b> (SLE, PAN)<br><b>5. Sickle cell crisis</b><br>Bilateral CRAO is rare and should prompt <b>aggressive systemic workup</b>, especially for GCA in patients >50." },
{ q: "What is the <b>RAPD</b> grading relevance in predicting CRVO outcome?", a: "The <b>severity of the RAPD (relative afferent pupillary defect)</b> in CRVO correlates with the <b>degree of retinal ischemia</b>.<br>• <b>Absent RAPD</b> → likely <b>non-ischemic</b> CRVO (better prognosis)<br>• <b>Present RAPD</b> (especially ≥0.9 log units) → likely <b>ischemic</b> CRVO (60% risk of NVG)<br>RAPD is the <b>single best clinical predictor</b> of ischemic vs non-ischemic CRVO at presentation." },
{ q: "What is <b>commotio retinae</b> (Berlin edema)?", a: "<b>Retinal whitening</b> (outer retinal disruption) following <b>blunt ocular trauma</b>.<br>Mechanism: Mechanical disruption of photoreceptor outer segments.<br><b>If involving the macula</b> → decreased VA (commotio retinae of the macula).<br><b>Peripheral commotio:</b> Often asymptomatic, resolves spontaneously.<br><b>OCT:</b> Hyperreflectivity and disruption of the photoreceptor layer.<br>No specific treatment; prognosis depends on severity of foveal involvement." },
{ q: "Summarize the <b>emergency management algorithm</b> for acute painless monocular vision loss.", a: "<b>1. Check VA, pupils (APD?), IOP</b><br><b>2. Dilated fundus exam:</b><br>→ <b>Pale retina + cherry-red spot</b> → CRAO → ocular massage, anterior chamber paracentesis, rule out GCA<br>→ <b>Blood & thunder</b> → CRVO → anti-VEGF, classify ischemic vs non-ischemic<br>→ <b>Elevated retina + break</b> → RRD → assess macula-on/off → surgery<br>→ <b>Cannot see fundus</b> → VH → B-scan US → PPV if RD<br>→ <b>Disc edema</b> → AION → check ESR/CRP → steroids if arteritic<br><b>3. Systemic workup</b> as indicated (carotid, cardiac, labs)" },
];
